- How does the solution change if edge weights represent communication delay instead of task duration?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions using prefix sums and a hashmap to achieve linear-time partition counting. This also exercises both bottom-up and top-down reasoning, and edge-case handling for K and negative numbers.

## Key Concepts

- Prefix sums
- Hash map DP
- Edge-case handling (K=0, negative numbers)

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPartitions(A, K) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const N = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  const map = new Map();
  map.set(0, 1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
    const key = prefix[i] - K;
    const ways = map.get(key) ?? 0;
    dp[i] = ways;
    const cur = (map.get(prefix[i]) ?? 0) + dp[i];
    map.set(prefix[i], cur % MOD);
  }
  return dp[N] % MOD;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to count partitions with an additional constraint, e.g., every block length <= L?
- What if A contains large numbers and K is dynamic per query?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of three key concepts: Trie mutations (insert/delete), weighted dynamic programming for word break problems, and the ability to implement both iterative and recursive approaches. It also evaluates understanding of handling dynamic dictionary updates and edge cases such as when no valid decomposition exists.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal weights for efficient prefix matching
- Dynamic insert/delete operations on Trie structure
- Weighted word-break DP with O(n × L) complexity where L = maxWordLen
- Both iterative bottom-up DP and memoized top-down DFS variants

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.endWeight = 0;
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class WeightedTrie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.maxWordLen = 0;
  }

  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
    node.endWeight = weight;
    this.maxWordLen = Math.max(this.maxWordLen, word.length);
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return;
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = false;
    node.endWeight = 0;
  }

  maxPartition(s) {
    const n = s.length;
    const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
    dp[n] = 0;

    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      let node = this.root;
      for (let j = i; j < Math.min(n, i + this.maxWordLen); j++) {
        const char = s[j];
        if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
        node = node.children.get(char);
        if (node.isEnd && dp[j + 1] !== -1) {
          dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], node.endWeight + dp[j + 1]);
        }
      }
    }
    return dp[0];
  }

  maxPartitionDFS(s, memo = {}) {
    if (s.length === 0) return 0;
    if (s in memo) return memo[s];

    let maxWeight = -1;
    let node = this.root;

    for (let i = 0; i < Math.min(s.length, this.maxWordLen); i++) {
      const char = s[i];
      if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
      node = node.children.get(char);

      if (node.isEnd) {
        const remaining = this.maxPartitionDFS(s.slice(i + 1), memo);
        if (remaining !== -1) {
          maxWeight = Math.max(maxWeight, node.endWeight + remaining);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[s] = maxWeight;
    return maxWeight;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem demonstrates how to model a constrained, single-use resource within a shortest-path framework. It tests understanding of state-space expansion and whether a candidate can adapt classic algorithms to modified constraints.

## Key Concepts
- **State-Space Expansion:** Augment each vertex with a binary flag indicating coupon usage
- **Layer 0 (coupon unused):** Can transition to layer 0 (full cost) or layer 1 (half cost)
- **Layer 1 (coupon used):** Can only transition to layer 1 (full cost only)
- **Path Reconstruction:** Track predecessor states to identify which edge receives the discount

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithCoupon(graph, s, t) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [Infinity, Infinity]);
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [null, null]);
  const pq = new PriorityQueue((a, b) => a.dist < b.dist);

  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.push({ u: s, used: 0, dist: 0 });

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const { u, used, dist: d } = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if (u === t) break;

    for (const { v, w } of graph[u]) {
      // Transition without using coupon
      if (d + w < dist[v][used]) {
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = { u, used };
        pq.push({ u: v, used, dist: dist[v][used] });
      }
      // Transition using coupon (only if not yet used)
      if (used === 0) {
        const newDist = d + w / 2;
        if (newDist < dist[v][1]) {
          dist[v][1] = newDist;
          prev[v][1] = { u, used, discounted: { from: u, to: v, weight: w } };
          pq.push({ u: v, used: 1, dist: newDist });
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const best = dist[t][0] < dist[t][1] ? 0 : 1;
  return { distance: dist[t][best], finalState: best };
}
```

